WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.214
Logging is an application data flow, not a
harmless print operation.

2
00:00:04.294 --> 00:00:07.496
Information leaves request handlers and
domain services,

3
00:00:07.576 --> 00:00:12.058
passes through serializers and queues, and
reaches storage, dashboards, alerts,

4
00:00:12.138 --> 00:00:13.291
or external vendors.

5
00:00:13.371 --> 00:00:17.725
That flow supports troubleshooting,
production monitoring, abuse detection,

6
00:00:17.805 --> 00:00:22.736
incident investigation, auditability,
compliance evidence, and performance analysis.

7
00:00:22.816 --> 00:00:25.505
Because it crosses trust and storage
boundaries,

8
00:00:25.585 --> 00:00:29.299
it deserves the same design review as an
API or database integration.

9
00:00:29.379 --> 00:00:32.709
Telemetry also creates a secondary copy of
application data.

10
00:00:32.789 --> 00:00:36.503
A single convenient object dump can
capture authorization headers,

11
00:00:36.583 --> 00:00:40.169
cookies, credentials, personal
information, customer content,

12
00:00:40.249 --> 00:00:43.450
internal identifiers, stack traces, and
network details.

13
00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:48.013
Those copies may be retained longer and
exposed to more operators than the original

14
00:00:48.093 --> 00:00:48.413
data.

15
00:00:48.493 --> 00:00:51.695
The engineering goal is therefore not
maximum collection.

16
00:00:51.775 --> 00:00:56.065
A useful event preserves enough context to
explain important behavior without

17
00:00:56.145 --> 00:01:00.819
turning observability into an uncontrolled
database of secrets and request content.

18
00:01:00.899 --> 00:01:03.461
Different signals require different
choices.

19
00:01:03.541 --> 00:01:06.935
Application and security events describe
discrete behavior.

20
00:01:07.015 --> 00:01:10.729
Metrics aggregate measurements and should
avoid unbounded labels.

21
00:01:10.809 --> 00:01:15.163
Traces connect work across services, often
carrying attributes at high volume.

22
00:01:15.243 --> 00:01:19.213
Audit records may need stable schemas and
stronger delivery, integrity,

23
00:01:19.293 --> 00:01:21.214
retention, and access guarantees.

24
00:01:21.294 --> 00:01:24.368
Diagnostic errors can contain deeper
technical detail,

25
00:01:24.448 --> 00:01:27.201
but that does not make them safe for every
audience.

26
00:01:27.281 --> 00:01:31.699
Treating all five as interchangeable
produces either weak evidence or excessive

27
00:01:31.779 --> 00:01:32.356
exposure.

28
00:01:32.436 --> 00:01:35.701
Begin with named events and explicit
fields: what happened,

29
00:01:35.781 --> 00:01:40.200
who or what initiated it, which resource
was affected, and what outcome occurred.

30
00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:44.506
Select the minimum fields that support a
defined operational or security use.

31
00:01:44.586 --> 00:01:48.236
Structure them consistently, exclude or
redact sensitive values,

32
00:01:48.316 --> 00:01:51.069
protect the pipeline, and test the emitted
output.

33
00:01:51.149 --> 00:01:55.120
In TypeScript, logging a project
identifier, actor identifier, action,

34
00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:56.672
and outcome is reviewable.

35
00:01:56.752 --> 00:01:58.994
Logging an entire Express request is not,

36
00:01:59.074 --> 00:02:03.620
because future framework or request
changes can silently expand what is collected.
